{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ובנבלתם לא תגעו", :translit "u-v'neveilatam lo tig'u", :gloss "and their carcasses you shall not touch"} {:he "את זה תאכלו", :translit "et zot tocheilu", :gloss "these you may eat"} {:he "נבילה מוסרחת", :translit "neveilah musrachat", :gloss "a putrid carcass"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut: the phrase 'these you may eat' (Deut 14:4) is juxtaposed with the prohibition of touching carcasses (Deut 14:8)."}], :frame {:text "The next chapter, Deut 15:19–23, opens with laws of firstborn animals and closes with the rule that a נבילה וטרפה may not be eaten but may be given to the ger (Deut 15:22–23). When a carcass becomes so decayed that it is unfit even for the ger, it no longer carries tum'ah at all (Mishnah Niddah 9:5, Rambam Hilkhot Shechitah 10:13, Rashba Torat HaBayit 6:2). The Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition of 'these you may eat' (Deut 14:4) with the carcass prohibition (Deut 14:8) as hinting at this halakhic scenario.", :sources ["Deut 14:4" "Deut 14:8" "Deut 15:19–23" "Mishnah Niddah 9:5" "Rambam Hilkhot Shechitah 10:13" "Rashba Torat HaBayit 6:2"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לא תגעו", :translit "lo tig'u", :gloss "you shall not touch"} {:he "חייב לטהר ברגל", :translit "chayav l'taheir b'regel", :gloss "obligated to purify on the festival"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "לא תגעו", :b "חייב לטהר ברגל"}], :frame nil}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut: the phrase 'these you may eat' (Deut 14:4) is juxtaposed with the prohibition of touching carcasses (Deut 14:8).", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:a-value 510, :b-value 509, :lens :standard, :equal? false, :kind :gematria, :b "חייב לטהר ברגל", :by :selah, :colel? true, :a "לא תגעו"}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:35.767127852Z", :model "glm-5.2"}